    "article": "**Pay Mix and OTE: The Architecture Behind Sales Compensation**\n\nWhen companies design sales compensation plans, two concepts sit at the center of every conversation: OTE and pay mix. Understanding how these two elements work together is essential for anyone who wants to analyze, model, or explain how salespeople are paid.\n\n**What Is OTE?**\n\nOTE stands for On-Target Earnings. It represents the total amount a salesperson expects to earn when they hit 100% of their quota. For example, if a sales representative has an OTE of $120,000, that is their expected annual compensation when performance meets the target exactly. OTE includes both fixed and variable components. It is not a guaranteed number — it is a target.\n\n**What Is Pay Mix?**\n\nPay mix describes the ratio between a salesperson's base salary and their variable pay (also called commission or incentive pay). This ratio is usually written as two numbers that add up to 100. A common pay mix for an inside sales role might be 60/40. This means 60% of OTE comes from base salary and 40% comes from variable pay.\n\nUsing our $120,000 OTE example: a 60/40 mix produces a base salary of $72,000 and a variable target of $48,000. If the salesperson exceeds quota, they can earn more than $48,000 in variable pay. If they miss quota, they earn less.\n\n**Why Pay Mix Matters**\n\nPay mix is not arbitrary. Companies choose it strategically based on several factors. The first factor is sales cycle length. Roles with long sales cycles — such as enterprise software deals that take six to twelve months to close — often carry a higher base salary. This protects the salesperson during months when no deals close. The second factor is role complexity. Technical sales engineers or solution consultants, who support the sales process but do not directly close deals, typically receive a more conservative mix like 70/30 or even 80/20. The third factor is market competition. In highly competitive talent markets, companies sometimes raise the base component to attract candidates who want income stability.\n\nOn the other hand, roles where the salesperson controls the full sales cycle — from prospecting to closing — often carry a more aggressive mix like 50/50. The logic is simple: if you close more, you earn more.\n\n**A Real-World Consideration**\n\nAccording to data from WorldatWork and Salesforce compensation benchmarks, the most common pay mix in B2B SaaS inside sales is between 60/40 and 70/30. However, enterprise field sales roles frequently use 50/50 mixes, reflecting the higher individual impact of each rep.\n\nFor revenue operations and data science professionals, pay mix is not just an HR decision. It directly affects how you model attainment distributions, calculate compensation expense, and forecast variable pay accruals.",
